% Channel flow — a library example (run it with F5; "Make a copy" to change it)
%
% Flow through a channel past a round post, by the lattice Boltzmann method (lbm2d, written out in script). The
% channel is 60 × 24 cells, closed by walls at the top and bottom, periodic left–right, driven by a body force
% (like a pressure drop). The post (radius 3.5 cells) sits a third of the way along. The canvas shows the velocity
% (pick a cell to read it), the dock plots the convergence. Lattice units: with τ = 0.8 the viscosity is 0.1; the
% Reynolds number on the post diameter is printed. Raise the force for faster flow (keep max |u| below 0.1), or
% draw your own obstacle into `solid`.
clf                                    % start with an empty canvas and dock
nx = 60; ny = 24;
solid = false(nx, ny);
solid(:, [1 ny]) = true;                        % walls
[I, J] = ndgrid(1:nx, 1:ny);
solid((I - 20).^2 + (J - 12.5).^2 <= 3.5^2) = true;  % the post
tau = 0.8; F = 1e-4;
r = lbm2d(solid, struct('tau', tau, 'force', [F 0], 'tol', 1e-5, 'maxSteps', 10000, 'every', 200));
umean = mean(r.ux(~isnan(r.ux)));
fprintf('mean velocity %.4g, Reynolds number on the post %.1f\n', umean, umean * 7 / r.nu);
speed = sqrt(r.ux' .^ 2 + r.uy' .^ 2);           % rows = y, for the heat map
speed(isnan(speed)) = 0;
imagesc(speed, 'title', 'Channel flow: speed');
